isplay: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">A typica=
l hiring pipeline at a startup or small company. The actual process, and nu=
mbers will vary on a role-by-role, company-by-company basis.</figcaption></=
div></figure>
<p>For companies that are very small or have minimal resources to spend on =
hiring, the hiring manager often does all the work. This includes the resum=
e screen, the screening call, and coordinating the onsite interviews.</p>
<p><strong>For companies with little technical expertise, you=E2=80=99ll al=
most certainly have to pass algorithmic coding challenges</strong> that are=
 often challenging. But why would a small, barely known company have coding=
 challenges nearly as difficult as larger tech companies have? It=E2=80=99s=
 because they don=E2=80=99t have in-house expertise to interview for these =
skills, and they buy a screening solution instead.</p>
<p>If the founder is non-technical and there aren=E2=80=99t other hands-on =
hiring managers, these companies will often =E2=80=9Coutsource=E2=80=9D tec=
hnical screening to an out-of-the-box solution like Hackerrank. Hackerrank =
and similar solutions are often built with Silicon Valley hiring in mind, m=
eaning the bar to pass can be difficult. What=E2=80=99s more, the non-techn=
ical hiring managers in these companies often use the score from the test a=
s the signal on how well you did. They won=E2=80=99t even look at your code=
: so a missing edge case could make you fail this screen.</p>
<p>Outside of major tech hubs, this kind of approach to screening software =
developers is more common than most people assume. It should be of little s=
urprise that the screening and the actual work you=E2=80=99d do are not sim=
ilar. Still, as annoying getting algorithmic challenges for a small company=
 can be, there=E2=80=99s an upside. Should you pass, you=E2=80=99ll probabl=
y be one of the first hands-on technical hires. If you=E2=80=99re passionat=
e about hiring, you will likely be able to shape the interview process for =
the better, from within the company.</p>
<p><strong>For companies with technical founders, the hiring process can be=
 one of the nicest ones.</strong> Your resume is almost always read by a te=
chnical hiring manager, who works with developers day-to-day. The first pho=
ne call is with the hiring manager themselves=E2=80=94most likely, your fut=
ure manager. The technical screens rarely have algorithmic challenges, and =
the onsite is shorter as well. There=E2=80=99s less competition, and you ar=
e more likely to get an offer in the end. So what=E2=80=99s the catch?</p>
<p>The catch is that these are small, lesser-known companies. They will alm=
ost certainly not sponsor visas. The compensation will almost certainly be =
below that of the large tech company. The company might seem like a really =
risky bet.</p>
<p>However, it=E2=80=99s precisely these reasons why these companies are se=
eing so few applications. And some of these assumptions might not be true. =
The compensation might be solid=E2=80=94perhaps it=E2=80=99s just not adver=
tised. The company might still sponsor visas=E2=80=94especially if they are=
 failing to attract even local talent. And while the company might be a ris=
ky bet, the risk could easily pay off.</p>
